<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="runner-credentials"
      updated="August 21, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"enrollment-keys", "Runner enrollment keys"},
        {"runner-tokens", "Per-runner tokens"},
        {"pack-credentials", "Pack and provider credentials"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="runner-credentials">
        <:dek>
          The credentials a runner fleet runs on: the enrollment key that registers hosts, the
          token each runner holds, and the provider credentials packs read.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <p class="mt-6 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        For every other emisar credential, and what pulling each one breaks, see <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/credentials"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Rotate and revoke credentials</.link>.
      </p>

      <ul class="mt-6 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Enrollment key</strong>
            (<code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emkey-enroll-…</code>) — registers a new runner. You mint it in the
            console: single-use for one host, reusable for a fleet template.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Per-runner token</strong>
            (<code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">rnrtok-…</code>) — what a registered runner connects with. Created at
            first connect and renewed automatically; you never type it.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Pack and provider credentials</strong>
            — your own secrets that pack actions use: a Nomad token, a database password. They
            live on the host and are never emisar credentials.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-6 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Each section below covers the rotation and revocation for one of these. Every rotation
        follows the same shape: create the new value, update whatever uses
        it, prove the new path with a real call, then revoke the old value and prove it now
        fails. The full seven-step version is in <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/credentials" <> "#sequence"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >the rotation sequence</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="enrollment-keys">Runner enrollment keys</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        An enrollment key registers new runners. It is not what keeps existing runners connected —
        each host exchanged its key for its own token on first connect.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Rotating a reusable provisioning key.</strong>
            Mint a new key under <a
              href={~p"/app/runners/keys"}
              class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Runners → Enrollment keys</a>. Update the template that sets
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">EMISAR_ENROLLMENT_KEY</code>
            in cloud-init, Packer, or the autoscaling startup script. Boot one host to prove
            enrollment with the new key. Then revoke the old key.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Revocation blocks registrations, not connections.</strong>
            After revoking, a host presenting that key is refused at registration. Runners
            already enrolled under it keep running on their own tokens and are unaffected.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Re-enrolling a host you keep.</strong>
            A runner stamps its cached token with a fingerprint of the key that minted it. Set the
            new key in
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/etc/emisar/runner.env</code>
            — or re-run the installer with the new <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">EMISAR_ENROLLMENT_KEY</code>,
            which writes it for you — and restart. The fingerprint no longer matches, so the
            runner registers again — under its configured <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">runner.id</code>, or its
            hostname — and replaces the cached token. No second runner identity appears.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/runner-fleet"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Manage the runner fleet</.link>
        covers key models, caps, and expiry;
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/autoscaling-fleets"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Autoscaling fleets</.link>
        covers the boot-time flow.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="runner-tokens">Per-runner tokens</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A runner token lasts 90 days and renews itself. On a connection at 60 days, the runner
        exchanges it for a successor — no host access or enrollment key needed — and the outgoing
        token remains valid for 24 hours after the swap, so a runner that dies before saving the
        successor can still reconnect.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A token that expires anyway — a long offline stretch, repeated refresh failures — is
        refused. The runner then discards it and re-registers with its stored enrollment key; a
        spent single-use key works only for the original identity, so otherwise give the host a
        fresh key.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        There is no separate token control. Disable or delete the runner identity in <a
          href={~p"/app/runners"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Runners</a>:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Disable is reversible.</strong>
            Use disable to take a runner out of service: the identity and token remain stored,
            the connection is refused while the host service keeps retrying, and enabling it
            restores service without host access.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Delete is terminal.</strong>
            The token stops working, the next connection is refused, and the service exits
            rather than retrying forever. Run and audit history for that runner is preserved.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">A suspected leak needs a new identity.</strong>
            Disabling and re-enabling hands the same token back — delete the runner and enroll
            the host again under a fresh enrollment key instead.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="pack-credentials">Pack and provider credentials</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A pack's credential — a Nomad token, a database password, a cloud key — is never an emisar
        credential. The runner reads it from its own environment. The control plane does not
        distribute or store it as configuration, and it cannot rotate the value. If an action
        prints the value and redaction misses it, the value can appear in run output.
      </p>
      <ol class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-300 list-decimal pl-6">
        <li>
          Rotate the value in the system that owns it — your secret manager, the provider's console.
        </li>
        <li>
          Update
          <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/etc/emisar/runner.env</code>
          on each affected host, keeping it mode <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">0600</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Restart the service. The environment is read at process start, so a reload of the pack
          catalog is not enough.
        </li>
        <li>Run one safe read-only action from that pack and confirm it succeeds.</li>
        <li>
          Revoke the old value at the provider, then run the same action again to confirm nothing still depends on it.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/runner-fleet"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Manage the runner fleet</.link>
        lists the variables a pack reads, and explains how the
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">inherit_env</code>
        allowlist decides what reaches an action.
      </p>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
